Yay! At first I considered writing posts such as these a bit self-
promoting ("ma, look what i did!"), but as I myself consider reading
such posts rather educationary and entertaining sometimes, I decided
what the hell. A warning, though: this post is rather lengthy ~~.
Hopefully this post might at least give some old gurus an opportunity
to scratch their gray beard with a smug smile on their face as they
read about my newbie antics.

I came into the world of NetHack quite recently, about 2=BD weeks ago,
and obviously ascended with very substantial help from various
spoilers (to the point that I spent about as much time playing as
reading about the game).

So, most of my previous characters were basic munchkin lawful
valkyries, and the ones that got farthest were usually killed by
stupid moves around some giant eels. I decided to try a barb for a
change, definitely the poison res would help in the early game.

The early game was not very eventful, except that I managed to get my
weapons thoroughly corroded on the first few levels. I aimed for
Sokoban first, to get a free useful item and some rings and wands that
usually clutter the place. After a very annoyingly spawned mimic
forced me to lose some luck, I was the happy owner of a brand new
amulet of reflection.

Next up: Minetown. There I found an expensive bag and lamp lying
around in a shop. To boot, both were blessed from the get-go. The lamp
yielded me a GDSM, and the BoH was much appreciated, as I have found
stashing quite tedious with no reliable means of traveling to it
quickly. Minetown was also the dying place of a former incarnation of
Jones, killed by "a hallucinogen-induced watch captain". Yeah, that
game didn't go as planned. Luckily, the captain had apparently gone
through rehab since and gotten his addiction under control, so I was
free to continue my voyage.

On my way down the levels, I found a shop with boots of speed and GoD.
Now, all I needed was a proper weapon... After some futile sacrificing
I decided that Set wasn't the best guy to ask for weapons after all
and continued my descent. A few levels down, a hobgoblin-filled throne
room actually yielded a wish! After some 15 minutes of spoiler
reading, I decided to get Frost Brand, and used it for the rest of the
game. Goodbye, thoroughly rusty thoroughly corroded two-handed sword.
Shortly afterwards, I was mowing down a throne room full of bugbears
and goblins, when I suddenly noticed I was standing next to some 3
dragons, an olog-hai, and other assorted "nice guys" thanks to a Titan
that had been lurking in a corner. After a short panic, I had the
sense to burn E on the square and take pot-shots at everyone until the
crowd thinned down.

My good luck continued; on the quest level, i leveled to lev13 and in
the next room found a wraith. Sure enough, it left a corpse, so I was
good to go on the quest, which was a breeze. Sure would prefer the
boatloads of giants from the valk quest to the boatload of useless
respawn-happy trolls, though. At least the quest is nice barbarian
role play; fight monsters with one hand, and gnaw on raw meat and
bones with the other. After the quest I had a chance encounter with a
tengu, and braved eating the corpse because i had a ring of TC; the
RNG gods still smiled on Jones and granted him intrinsic TC. Before I
went further, I genocided L and master mind flayers (yeah, should have
blessed that other scroll).

With some means of levitation in my disposal, I could now say hi to
Medusa and his shield of reflection, and go play some mastermind. I
think more monsters died of laughter to the sight of a blindfolded
(eel-radar) dude playing random tunes with an old bugle than to the
flapping of the drawbridge. I didn't really know what to wish for with
the castle wand in addition to some charging scrolls and a lifeguard
amulet, so I just stashed it after those and went on. Side note: one
of the scariest moments of this char occurred here, as at first I was
absent-mindedly clearing the moat, and didn't notice an eel had swung
itself around me, but luckily I killed it with my next shot. Pheeeew.

In the valley, I decided to cancel a bunch of useless scrolls and wish
up a marker to get some magic mapping. Apparently Jones became scared
of water after the run-in with the eel so he didn't want to blank the
scrolls by taking a dive...

Halfway through Gehennom I noticed that it probably wasn't very wish-
efficient to map every level with a written scroll as I go. Another
rookie sidestep came on Juiblex's swamp. The man itself was easily
dispatched, but after that I decided to make a shortcut by digging
through the floor instead of finding the downstairs. Whoops. After a
nose dive and a renewed fear of water I discovered most of my scrolls
blanked in my (non-greased) BoH. Nevertheless, from this point on the
mapping went quite easily, found the VB, killed Vlad, and prepared to
take on the big guys. Another magic marker was wished to enchant some
armor, and a poly wand was wasted on piles of random wands and amulets
trying to get wands of deaths and lifesaving amulets. In hindsight, I
should have gathered the dozen of unihorns lying around everywhere and
tried to get some markers instead.

From spoiler-knowledge, I had developed a deep fear of the Wiz-man;
had never gotten this far yet with any character. Turned out, he
wasn't such a tough guy after all, when pre-emptively zapped with a
WoD found at his own zoo. Should teach him to not give such toys to
his pets. Levelport to VB, a dash to the temple and a cursed level
gain pot later I began the ascent. This mysterious force thing sure is
a bitch. In the valley, I heard someone say "Thou thinkst thou could
kill me?". Another zap proved that I did not only think it, I could do
it as well. Same thing happened when I entered the plane of earth.

From spoilers, I gathered that the plane of air is the hard one, and
the rest is rather easy. Turns out it was the easiest one to me;
thanks to conflict and being very fast I never dropped below max-20 hp
and was never engulfed. And damn, the fire one is tedious as all hell,
thanks to the constant "thanks for the info" messages. Didn't help
that my portal detection methods were 2 wands of SDD and of course the
portal was in the last corner.

This was nothing compared to the water one, though. At first I
randomly walked around zapping my wands, until they ran out of
charges. Then I had the sense to start wielding the amulet. After an
hour or two of more aimless wandering, I discovered a charging scroll
in my bag for one of the SDD wands. Then I waited until I got the
amulet to being "hot". "Ha, portal, I have you now!", I said and
zapped the wand. I suddenly had a Fire Elemental beside me, of all
things, and nothing else seemed to happen. After much confusion and
spoiler-assuring that no, charged wands of SDD actually do not spawn
random fire elementals when zapped on the plane of water, I finally
realized that the wands just don't work when the portal is a moving
one. Sigh. Feeling like an idiot seems to work though, because shortly
after i randomly managed to find the portal. (Side note #2: I had
genocided ; before the plane, luckily)

With a heart rate nearing 250 I prepared for the greatest of all
challenges, the super-almighty astral plane inhabited by foes able to
cause apocalyptic mayhem and where the mightiest minions of all the
divine and diabolic deities walk side by side ready to smite any
foolish mortal that dares disturb their works! And what happened?
Without using a single item I ran past Famine and his entourage to
find the altar of Set on the first try and ascended to demigod-hood-
ness. Boy, talk about anti-climatic.

Turns out the things I feared most, Rodney and the Astral Plane,
happened to be trivial and effortless in this game. What does this
teach us? I have no idea.

(Sorry, no usual endgame litanies, didn't store them as I didn't think
I'd do a post such as this at the time)


